# Fingerprints of gitleaks findings reviewed and confirmed to be false positives.
#
# Format: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>. Fingerprints pin one specific occurrence rather than
# disabling a rule, so a genuine key committed elsewhere, or later in the same file, is still
# caught. Each entry needs a reason.
#
# These reference commits on purpose: the CI job scans history, not just the working tree, so a
# finding stays reported even after the line changes. That is correct for secret scanning, since
# anything once committed remains retrievable.
#
# MAINTENANCE NOTE: because the commit hash is part of the fingerprint, rewriting history
# invalidates every entry here and the findings come back. If history is ever rewritten again,
# re-run `gitleaks git -v .` and replace the hashes below with the new ones.

